# ==== T-FMT-C · a trailing comment ON the `else:` header stays on the header
#
# `else:  # note` was the one clause header that dropped its trailing comment
# into the branch body, where `format_block_stmts` re-emitted it as a LEADING
# comment on the branch's first statement — so a note about the `else` came out
# reading as a note about whatever happened to come first inside it. The
# misattribution class T-FMT-A retired, surviving at the sites nobody censused.
#
# It survived because of an ASYMMETRY, not an oversight in the abstract: `elif`
# fires a header hook and so does `case`, so those two positions were right;
# only `else` had none. EVERY indented-`else` site now goes through ONE producer,
# `Formatter::emit_else_header`, which writes the header, fires the hook and ends
# the line — one implementation, so the next `else` position cannot ship without
# it. The exact site count is pinned by `formatter_sibling_loops_hook_pairing`
# in tests/lints.rs, not restated here where it would rot.
#
# SITE AXIS (6 statement cells): `if`-`else` · statement-match `else` · `for`
#   `else` · `while` `else` · `meta match` `else` · expression-match `else`. The
#   last one reaches its body through `format_arm_body`, which is where the
#   header comment is placed for every arm-shaped position.
#
# PLUS 3 ITEM-LEVEL cells (`meta if` over ITEMS): the `meta if` header itself,
#   its `elif`, and its `else`. Same class, one layer up — the nested-item loop
#   re-emitted a header's trailing comment as a LEADING comment on the branch's
#   first DEFINITION. The item-level `else` has no clause span, so its anchor is
#   derived by walking back from the first item to the colon.
#
# PLUS 4 ARM-BODY SHAPE cells (`case`/`else`/`catch` with an author `do:`, and
#   the inline sibling). These are the third body shape, and they are the reason
#   `format_arm_body` — not the call site — decides where the header comment
#   goes. An author `do:` belongs to the HEADER's line, so the comment is emitted
#   after ` do:`; firing the hook before it spells `else:  # note do:` and eats
#   the keyword, which is ACCEPT/REJECT-relevant (`else: do: ^b` is rejected
#   where `else: ^b` compiles). These cells exist because an earlier version of
#   this file claimed the shape was "excluded by construction" while the
#   exclusion arm had NO fixture at all — flipping it left every gate green.
#
# ⚠ INLINE bodies get the third treatment, not the header hook. A comment emitted
# at the header of `else: stmt` lands ahead of the statement that shares the line,
# and the output does not re-parse — those are CLAIMED at the header and emitted
# after the body, by `format_inline_suite` for statement suites and by
# `format_arm_body` for arm-shaped positions.
#
# RED pre-fix (recorded 2026-08-11, break-and-restore per cell): every `else`
#   cell moved its comment down one line and one indent level into the branch
#   body. The author-`do:` cells were worse and differed by position — the `case`
#   one re-emitted the comment as a TRAILING comment on the branch's LAST
#   statement, the `else` and `catch` ones as a LEADING comment on the FIRST.

void if_else(int x):
    if x == 1:
        print(1)
    else:    # the fallback branch
        print(2)

void stmt_match_else(int x):
    match x:
        case 1:
            print(1)
        else:    # everything that is not 1
            print(2)

void for_else():
    for i in 0..3:
        print(i)
    else:    # ran to completion
        print(9)

void while_else():
    while false:
        print(1)
    else:    # never entered the loop
        print(2)

String meta_match_else[T]():
    meta match typename(T):
        case "int":
            return "i"
        else:    # every other type
            return "o"

int expr_match_else(int x):
    int r = match x:
        case 1:
            10
        else:    # the expression-position clause
            20
    return r

meta if platform() == "macos":    # the mac branch
    int backend_id():
        return 1
elif platform() == "windows":    # the windows branch
    int backend_id():
        return 2
else:    # every other platform
    int backend_id():
        return 3

int rem_or_throw(int a, int b):
    return a %! b

int arm_body_do_shapes(int x):
    int viacase = match x:
        case 1: do:    # author `do:` on a CASE arm
            int a = 1
            a + 1
        else:
            0
    int viaelse = match x:
        case 9:
            9
        else: do:    # author `do:` on an ELSE clause
            int b = 2
            b + 1
    int viacatch = rem_or_throw(x, 0)! catch (e): do:    # author `do:` on a CATCH arm
        int c = 3
        c
    int inline_sibling = match x:
        case 9:
            9
        else: 4    # the INLINE sibling — claimed, then emitted after the body
    return viacase + viaelse + viacatch + inline_sibling

void main():
    if_else(1)
    stmt_match_else(1)
    for_else()
    while_else()
    print(meta_match_else[int]())
    print(expr_match_else(1))
    print(backend_id())
    print(arm_body_do_shapes(1))
